refore the \\(n-1\\) sunny lines together can cover at most \\(n-1\\) points\n   of each column.  Consequently each column contains at least one uncovered\n   point, contradicting the hypothesis that all points of \\(T_{n}\\) are\n   covered.\n\n   The same argument works when the unique forbidden line is a column or an\n   antidiagonal, because the roles of the three families are symmetric.\n   Hence at least two forbidden lines are required. ∎\n\n4. **Corollary 1 (upper bound for sunny lines).**  \n   From Lemma 2 we obtain  \n\n   \\[\n   k\\le n-2 .\n   \\tag{2}\n   \\]\n\n5. **Lemma 3 (a single sunny line is impossible).**  \n   For every \\(n\\ge 3\\) there is **no** configuration of \\(n\\) lines that\n   covers \\(T_{n}\\) and contains exactly one sunny line.\n\n   *Proof.*  Assume a configuration with exactly one sunny line \\(L\\) and\n   \\(n-1\\) forbidden lines.  By Lemma 2 there must be at least two forbidden\n   lines; hence the remaining \\(n-2\\) forbidden lines belong to at most two\n   of the three families (rows, columns, antidiagonals).  Without loss of\n   generality suppose they are rows and columns.  Then there is at least one\n   antidiagonal \\(D_{s}\\) that contains **no** forbidden line.  The antidiagonal\n   \\(D_{s}\\) consists of \\(s-1\\) points of \\(T_{n}\\) (all with distinct rows\n   and columns).  By Lemma 1 the sunny line \\(L\\) can meet \\(D_{s}\\) in at most\n   one point, while the remaining \\(n-2\\) forbidden lines are parallel to\n   rows or columns and therefore intersect \\(D_{s}\\) in at most one point\n   each.  Hence at most \\(n-1\\) points of \\(D_{s}\\) can be covered, but\n   \\(|D_{s}\\cap T_{n}|=s-1\\ge n\\) for the largest antidiagonal\n   (\\(s=n+1\\)).  This contradiction shows that a configuration with a single\n   sunny line cannot exist. ∎\n\n6. **Conclusion.**  \n   Lemma 2 forces \\(k\\le n-2\\); Lemma 3 rules out the case \\(k=1\\).  For\n   \\(k\\ge 2\\) we would need at most \\(n-2\\) forbidden lines