agonal.  \n   Each of the three families contains exactly \\(n\\) members (there are \\(n\\) rows, \\(n\\) columns and \\(n\\) antidiagonals).  \n   Consequently, with at most one forbidden line the total number of points that can be covered is at most  \n\n   \\[\n   \\underbrace{n}_{\\text{points on the forbidden line}}\n   \\;+\\;\n   (n-1)\\times n_{\\text{(max one per row for each sunny line)}}\n   \\;=\\;n^{2}.\n   \\]\n\n   However \\(|T_{n}|=\\dfrac{n(n+1)}{2}<n^{2}\\) for \\(n\\ge 3\\); the inequality does **not** give a contradiction.  \n   The stronger restriction comes from the *other* two families that the forbidden line does **not** belong to.  \n   Suppose the forbidden line is a row (i.e. \\(x=1\\)). Then every column \\(C_{b}\\) contains exactly \\(n\\) points of \\(T_{n}\\).  \n   A sunny line can intersect a given column in at most one point, so the \\(n-1\\) sunny lines can cover at most \\(n-1\\) points of each column.  \n   Hence each column contains at least one uncovered point, contradicting the hypothesis that all points of \\(T_{n}\\) are covered.  \n   The same argument works when the forbidden line is a column or an antidiagonal.  \n   Therefore at least two lines of \\(\\mathcal L\\) must be forbidden, and the number of sunny lines satisfies  \n\n   \\[\n   k\\le n-2 .\n   \\tag{1}\n   \\]\n\n3. **Sufficient condition \u2013 construction for every admissible \\(k\\).**  \n\n   We prove by induction on \\(n\\) that for each integer \\(k\\) with \\(0\\le k\\le n-2\\) there exists a family of \\(n\\) distinct lines covering all points of \\(T_{n}\\) and containing exactly \\(k\\) sunny lines.\n\n   *Base \\(n=3\\).*  \n   The set \\(T_{3}\\) consists of the six points  \n\n   \\[\n   (1,1),(1,2),(1,3),(2,1),(2,2),(3,1).\n   \\]\n\n   - \\(k=0\\): take the three forbidden lines  \n     \\[\n     L_{1}:x=1,\\qquad L_{2}:y=1,\\qquad L_{3}:x+y=4 .\n     \\]\n   - \\(k=1\\): keep \\(L_{1}\\) and \\(L_{2}\\) and replace \\(L_{3}\\) by the sunny line \\(L_{3}':y=2x\\) (which passes through \\((1,2)\\)).  \n   In each case the three lines are distinct and together contain all six points.  \n   Thus the statement holds for \\(n=3\\).\n\n   *Inductive step.*  \n   Assume the statement true for some \\(n\\ge 3\\).  \n   Let \\(k\\) be an integer with \\(0\\le k\\le n-1\\). We shall construct a suitable family for \\((n+1,k)\\).\n\n   **Case 1.** \\(k\\le n-2\\).  \n   By the induction hypothesis there exists a family \\(\\mathcal F_{n}\\) of \\(n\\) lines covering \\(T_{n}\\) with exactly \\(k\\) sunny lines.  \n   Add the forbidden line  \n\n   \\[\n   L_{\\text{new}}:x+y=n+2 .\n   \\tag{2}\n   \\]\n\n   The line (2) contains precisely the new points of \\(T_{n+1}\\) that are not already in \\(T_{n}\\), namely the points \\((a,b)\\) with \\(a+b=n+2\\).  \n   No point of \\(T_{n}\\) lies on \\(L_{\\text{new}}\\), so the previously covered points remain covered.  \n   The resulting family \\(\\mathcal F_{n+1}:=\\mathcal F_{n}\\cup\\{L_{\\text{new}}\\}\\) consists of \\(n+1\\) distinct lines, still has exactly \\(k\\) sunny lines, and covers all of \\(T_{n+1}\\).\n\n   **Case 2.** \\(k=n-1\\) (the only value not covered by the induction hypothesis).  \n   Construct the following \\(n+1\\) lines:\n\n   * the two forbidden lines \\(x=1\\) and \\(y=1\\);\n   * for each integer \\(t\\) with \\(2\\le t\\le n\\) the sunny line  \n\n     \\[\n     L_{t}:\\; y=(t-1)(x-1)+t .\n     \\tag{3}\n     \\]\n\n   The line \\(L_{t}\\) passes through the point \\((1,t)\\) (which lies on the vertical line \\(x=1\\)) and through the point \\((t,n+2-t)\\); the latter point belongs to \\(T_{n+1}\\) because \\(t+(n+2-t)=n+2\\le (n+1)+1\\).  \n   The slopes \\((t-1)\\) are all different from \\(0,\\infty,-1\\); hence each \\(L_{t}\\) is sunny and the \\(n-1\\) lines \\(\\{L_{t}\\mid 2\\le t\\le n\\}\\) are pairwise distinct.\n\n   The set of points covered by the \\(n+1\\) lines is exactly \\(T_{n+1}\\):\n   * Points with \\(a=1\\) lie on the vertical line \\(x=1\\);\n   * Points with \\(b=1\\) lie on the horizontal line \\(y=1\\);\n   * Any remaining point \\((a,b)\\) has \\(a\\ge 2,\\;b\\ge 2\\) and satisfies \\(a+b\\le n+2\\).  \n     Write \\(t:=b\\) (so \\(2\\le t\\le n\\)). Then \\((a,b)=(a,t)\\) lies on the line \\(L_{t}\\) because solving (3) for \\(x\\) gives \\(x=1+\\dfrac{b-t}{t-1}=a\\).\n\n   Thus the \\(n+1\\) lines cover all points of \\(T_{n+1}\\) and contain exactly \\(n-1\\) sunny lines (the \\(L_{t}\\)\u2019s).  \n   Finally, delete the forbidden line \\(x+y=n+2\\) (which is parallel to the antidiagonal and therefore forbidden) from this family.  \n   The remaining \\(n\\) lines still cover every point of \\(T_{n+1}\\) because the points that were on the deleted line are precisely those with \\(a+b=n+2\\); each such point \\((a,b)\\) also belongs to the sunny line \\(L_{b}\\) (since \\(b\\) satisfies \\(2\\le b\\le n\\)).  \n   Hence we obtain a configuration for \\((n+1,n-1)\\) with exactly \\(n-1\\) sunny lines.\n\n   The induction is complete: for every \\(n\\ge 3\\) and every \\(k\\) with \\(0\\le k\\le n-2\\) a suitable family exists.\n\n4. **Conclusion.**  \n   Lemma\u202f1 gives the necessary condition \\(k\\le n-2\\); the inductive construction shows that this condition is also sufficient. Therefore the set of admissible values of \\(k\\) is precisely  \n\n   \\[\n   \\boxed{\\{0,1,2,\\dots ,n-2\\}} .\n   \\]"
